-- D8's persistence path: the encoding, the compatibility policy, and the store.
--
-- WI-A13, stage 6, second commit. D8:
--
--   "Programs use a deterministic, diffable encoding with an explicit
--    compatibility policy. The implementation plan may select the encoding and
--    storage path... A schema migration must either preserve old-program
--    decoding or provide a pinned runner/artifact; silently reinterpreting an
--    old program is forbidden... Large exact payloads may live in a CI artifact
--    addressed by digest; a digest without retained bytes is not sufficient for
--    replay."
--
-- Nothing wrote a program to disk before this commit. The encoding and the
-- storage path are both selected here, and the four choices that were open are
-- recorded below because each admitted a second answer that type-checks.
--
-- ===========================================================================
-- CHOICE 1 — LINE-ORIENTED TEXT, NOT JSON, AND THE REASON IS "DIFFABLE"
-- ===========================================================================
--
-- `ports.ail`'s payload codecs use `std/json`, and reusing them here was the
-- obvious move. It is the wrong one, and the failure is quiet: `std/json`'s
-- `encode` emits ONE LINE. That is perfectly deterministic — the same program
-- encodes byte-identically every time — and it is not diffable at all, because
-- changing one field of one interaction rewrites the only line in the file. A
-- reviewer reading `git diff` on a regenerated program sees the whole artifact
-- replaced and cannot see what moved.
--
-- D8 asks for BOTH properties and the plan owns the trade. Line-oriented text
-- gives both: determinism because every value is written in a fixed order by a
-- pure function of the record with no map iteration anywhere, and diffability
-- because a one-field change touches exactly two lines — the field's own line
-- and the digest. `scenario_diffable` asserts that as a number, on three
-- different field positions, rather than asserting it as a property name.
--
-- The large variable-length fields — the request projection, the outcome
-- payload, and each stream chunk — get their OWN lines rather than sharing the
-- interaction's line. Those are the fields most likely to change and the ones
-- that can be long; sharing a line with them would make every payload edit look
-- like an identity edit in a diff.
--
-- ===========================================================================
-- CHOICE 2 — THE DIGEST IS IN THE ARTIFACT, AND THE BYTES ARE ALWAYS THERE
-- ===========================================================================
--
-- D8 permits a large payload to live in a CI artifact "addressed by digest" and
-- then closes the door the same sentence opened: "a digest without retained
-- bytes is not sufficient for replay". So a digest is a NAME here and never a
-- REPRESENTATION. `artifact_bytes` always carries the whole program; the digest
-- is a line inside it, computed over the body, and `decode_artifact` verifies
-- it. There is no encoded form of a program that is a reference to bytes stored
-- somewhere else, and `DigestMismatch` is what a truncated or edited artifact
-- produces instead of a program that decodes to something slightly different.
--
-- ===========================================================================
-- CHOICE 3 — SITE 22: THE PATH IS THE IDENTITY, THE KEY IS THE DIGEST
-- ===========================================================================
--
-- D8 names a preserved failure by (generator_id, generator_version, seed), and
-- cluster 11 measured that this triple is NOT UNIQUE. `seed_state` is
-- `in_range(salt_hash("${id}/${version}") + seed)` — the version hash and the
-- seed are ADDED, hence interchangeable — so version "2" at seed s is
-- byte-identical to version "1" at seed s+1, confirmed across all 259 adjacent
-- pairs, twice. `test_a_version_bump_is_currently_only_a_seed_offset` in
-- `dst_generator` pins the defect deliberately.
--
-- THE DECISION TAKEN HERE IS TO RECORD THE COLLISION AND KEY ON THE DIGEST,
-- AND `seed_state` IS LEFT ALONE. Not because the repair is expensive — it is,
-- it remaps the whole stream and moves stage 4's pinned seeds 9, 13 and 94,
-- each of which has an asserted reason — but because THE REPAIR WOULD NOT MAKE
-- THE TRIPLE A KEY:
--
--   D8's own reproducibility promise is conditioned on the recorded execution
--   manifest and profile — "Repeating discovery under the recorded execution
--   manifest/profile and seed must reproduce the same resolved program". The
--   program is therefore a function of (manifest/profile, id, version, seed),
--   and the triple omits the manifest. Two runs at one triple under two
--   manifests are two different programs TODAY, with or without site 22. So a
--   store that filed under the triple and believed it unique would be wrong for
--   a second, independent reason that fixing `seed_state` does not touch.
--
-- Both facts point the same way, so the store does not file under a key it
-- believes to be unique:
--
--   * `artifact_path` is derived from the triple, because a STABLE, human-
--     navigable path is what makes choice 1's diffability worth anything: the
--     same logical program lands at the same path across regenerations, so
--     `git diff` shows the change. A content-addressed path would put every
--     revision at a new name and there would be nothing to diff.
--   * `artifact_identity` is the DIGEST, and it is the only thing in this module
--     described as unique. A corpus deduplicates on it. Two artifacts at
--     different paths sharing an identity are site 22's aliasing made visible
--     rather than counted twice — which is the harm, and it is A15's: a corpus
--     holding (v1, seed 4) and (v2, seed 3) has one program's worth of coverage
--     while reporting two.
--   * `persist_program` REFUSES to overwrite a path whose existing artifact has
--     a different digest. The path is not unique, so the store checks instead of
--     assuming. Writing the same program twice is idempotent; writing a
--     different one at the same triple is `PersistCollision`, by name.
--
-- ===========================================================================
-- CHOICE 4 — THE DECODER HAS NO FIELD DEFAULTS, AND THAT IS STRUCTURAL
-- ===========================================================================
--
-- Cluster 9 cost this project a decoder that returned fallbacks for every
-- field: an out-of-scope constructor bound as a fresh variable, the arm became
-- irrefutable, `ailang check` was clean, and every field read back as its
-- default. That defect is THIS module's exact subject matter, so the decode is
-- two phases and the second is only reachable when the first passed:
--
--   VALIDATE — every required tag is present exactly once, every tag is known,
--     every line has its declared arity, every int field parses, every escape
--     is well-formed. Anything else is a `DecodeRefusal` naming the line.
--   PROJECT — read the fields. A lookup here cannot miss, because phase one
--     enumerated the tags from `required_header_tags()` and found them all.
--
-- The residual risk is a field the ENCODER writes and `required_header_tags()`
-- omits: phase one would not miss it, phase two would read "" for it, and both
-- halves type-check. That is S7's record-level form, and the two guards against
-- it are the field-by-field round trip with every field holding a DISTINCT
-- value (`scenario_round_trip`), and the Makefile's manifest-arity guard, which
-- counts `ExecutionManifest`'s declared fields against the codec's tags.
--
-- ===========================================================================
-- THE COMPATIBILITY POLICY IS A FILE, NOT A PARAGRAPH
-- ===========================================================================
--
-- `scripts/dst/fixtures/execution-program-v1.artifact` is a frozen specimen,
-- checked into the tree at the moment this encoding landed. THERE IS NO TARGET
-- THAT REGENERATES IT and that is the point rather than an omission — stage 5
-- deleted its sweep script after use for the same reason. A compatibility
-- policy needs a frozen artifact from the past or it tests nothing, and this
-- project had no past, so one is manufactured deliberately.
--
-- Encoder and decoder are written in the same commit and agree BY CONSTRUCTION,
-- so every round-trip row would pass against a policy that exists only as
-- prose. The specimen is what makes the clause executable: the current build
-- must decode those bytes correctly or refuse them by name, and if a future
-- edit breaks the decode the row goes red and the author must add a v1 decode
-- path or bump the version and pin a runner. Regenerating the file is the one
-- response that is not available, because it destroys the evidence.
--
-- Per S8's complement, a specimen certifies exactly the shapes it CONTAINS, so
-- it carries every shape the schema admits and `scenario_specimen_coverage`
-- asserts that against `all_interaction_kinds()` rather than against a list
-- written at the assertion site.
--
-- The second specimen, `execution-program-v0.artifact`, carries a schema
-- version this build does not know. It exists so the fail-closed half is tested
-- against real bytes rather than against a hand-built record: an unknown schema
-- is `UnknownSchemaVersion`, by name, pointing at the pinned runner.

